# SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <getopt.h>

extern char *optarg;
extern int optind, opterr, optopt;

struct option {
  const char *name;
  int has_arg;  // no_argument 0, required_argument 1, optional_argument 2
  int *flag;
  int val;
};

int getopt(int argc, char *const argv[], const char *options);
int getopt_long(int argc, char *const argv[], const char *options,
                const struct option *longopts, int *longindex);
int getopt_long_only(int argc, char *const argv[], const char *options,
                     const struct option *longopts, int *longindex);
```

`getopt` and its variables are also declared by `<unistd.h>`.

# DESCRIPTION

Each call returns the next option in <argv>, from `argv[optind]` on, and -1 at the first operand, at `--` (which it passes), or at the end.
<options> lists the option characters; one followed by `:` takes an argument, the rest of its word or the next word, and one followed by `::` takes an optional one, the rest of its word only.
Options may be grouped (`-vx`).
The argument is left in `optarg`, and `optind` is the index of the next word.

An option not in <options> returns `?` and leaves it in `optopt`; a missing argument returns `?`, or `:` when <options> begins with `:`.
Each prints a message to `stderr`, `<program>: <what>`, unless `opterr` is 0 or <options> begins with `:`.
A leading `+` or `-` in <options> is accepted and ignored.

`getopt_long` also takes `--name`, `--name=value` and `--name value` for each entry of <longopts>, which ends with an entry whose name is null; a unique prefix of a name is taken for it.
The entry's <has_arg> says whether it takes an argument.
For a match it stores the entry's index in <longindex>, if that is not null, and returns <val>, or, when <flag> is not null, stores <val> there and returns 0.
An unknown, ambiguous or wrongly given long option returns `?` with a message.
`getopt_long_only` takes long options after a single `-` too, and a single character there that names no long option as the short option.

Setting `optind` to 0 starts a new walk.

